<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Excited!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay messages from teacher to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures to relay the meaning of emotion-related language to peers (e.g., smiling to show 'I'm happy.').<br>Can use gestures and miming to relay information about basic needs (e.g., rubbing the stomach to show 'I'm hungry.').<br>Can interpret and convey simple 'I'm...' statements using body language (e.g., pretending to sleep to indicate 'I'm tired.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e7eb;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Feelings Song" with TPR. Students sing along and perform gestures for 'happy,' 'sad,' 'tired,' 'hungry,' 'excited.' Teacher asks, "How are you today?" and encourages students to respond with gestures and "I'm [emotion]." For example, teacher says, "I'm excited!" and jumps up and down. Students imitate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to mediate a message using gestures. Teacher whispers to one student, "Tell your friend you are hungry." The student then rubs their tummy and points to a peer. The teacher then models how to interpret and say, "Oh! You are hungry!" The teacher then models how to show an emotion and say "I'm excited!" (with exaggerated facial expression and tone) and points to their throat for the /e/ sound and the beginning of the word for the /h/ sound. Teacher explicitly demonstrates using body language to convey meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a smiling child) "Is this child sad or happy?" (Expecting: "Happy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher rubs tummy) "What do I need? Am I thirsty or hungry?" (Expecting: "Hungry!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards of children displaying different emotions (happy, sad, tired, hungry, excited). Teacher's example of the "Emotion Faces Chart" from the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing emotions and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm tired.' Recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'H' in 'happy').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large "Emotion Faces Chart" (a simple version with drawings and words like 'happy', 'sad', 'excited'). Teacher points to a face and says, "This face is happy. I'm happy!" and smiles. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher uses flashcards for new words (thirsty, calm, sleepy, sick, warm, angry). For each, the teacher shows the flashcard, acts out the emotion/need, and says, "I'm [emotion/need]." Students repeat the phrase and mimic the gesture/face.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word 'happy' on the chart and highlights the letter 'H', saying its sound /h/. Then for 'excited', highlights 'E' and says /e/.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to 'I'm tired!'" (Teacher says the phrase, students point to the correct picture/face on the chart).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me 'I'm excited!'" (Teacher says the phrase, students perform the gesture/face).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Relay messages from teacher to peers using gestures and simple sentences. Grammar: Simple sentences ('I'm happy.'), WH-questions ('How are you?'), I'm + adjective (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, calm, sleepy, excited, sick, warm, angry). Vocabulary: feel, need, want, rest, play, smile, frown, hug, help. Pronunciation: /h/ in 'happy', /e/ in 'excited'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing 'H' and 'E'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Whisper & Show":</b> The teacher whispers an emotion/need (e.g., "I'm hungry," "I'm tired," "I'm excited") to one student. That student must then use only gestures and facial expressions to relay this message to a partner. The partner then tries to guess and say, "You are [emotion/need]!" Teacher ensures /h/ and /e/ sounds are clear when speaking.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Emotion Relay Race":</b> Students are in small groups. The teacher holds up an emotion flashcard or whispers a feeling. The first student in each group must quickly act out the emotion. The second student then points to the corresponding emotion on a small 'Emotion Faces Chart' (provided by teacher) and says "I'm [emotion]!" or "He/She is [emotion]!" This encourages mediating between visual and verbal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "How are you, friend?":</b> Students work in pairs with their own (or teacher-provided simple) 'Emotion Faces Chart'. One student asks, "How are you?" The other student chooses an emotion from their chart, points to it, and says "I'm [emotion]," using gestures to reinforce the meaning (e.g., smiling for 'happy', rubbing stomach for 'hungry'). They then swap roles. This activity practices expressing and mediating feelings.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group "Emotion Chart Share and Tell." Students will use their self-created "Emotion Faces Chart" (21st Century Skills Project) to mediate feelings and needs to their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups (3-4 students). Each student brings out their "Emotion Faces Chart" they have been working on.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Today, you will use your chart to help your friends understand feelings. If I say 'happy,' you show 'happy' on your chart and make a happy face!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Mediation Challenge":</b> The teacher calls out an emotion/need (e.g., "Show your friend 'I'm excited!'"). Each student, acting as a mediator, points to the corresponding emotion on their chart and uses gestures (e.g., jumping for 'excited') and body language to convey the meaning to their group members. They can also say, "This is excited!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group members then try to guess the feeling if the mediator only uses gestures and the chart, or confirm their understanding. For example, if one student shows 'I'm tired' with their chart and by pretending to sleep, another student might say "Oh, you are tired! I understand!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, facilitating interaction, providing target vocabulary, and prompting students to use gestures and their charts to explain or relay information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, students will present their "Emotion Faces Chart" to the teacher or a small group. The teacher will prompt them with a feeling or a simple need (e.g., "Tell me how you feel when you want to play," or "Show me 'I'm happy' using your chart and actions"). The student must use their chart and gestures to mediate the meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Gestures/Miming:</b> Can the student clearly convey meaning (e.g., rubbing stomach for 'hungry', yawning for 'tired')?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Emotion Faces Chart:</b> Does the student effectively use their chart to support conveying the emotion?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the student attempt to use 'I'm [emotion]'? (e.g., 'I'm excited', 'I'm happy').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Is the pronunciation of words like 'happy' (/h/) and 'excited' (/e/) understandable?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (during group activity):</b> Did the student attempt to mediate/explain to peers?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will draw a simple happy face or a sad face on a small paper to show how they feel about using their "Emotion Faces Chart" today to share feelings. (e.g., "Did you feel happy showing your feelings chart?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What feeling did you like to show best with your chart and your body?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did your friends understand your feelings when you showed them?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific mediation activities worked well for Kinder students? Were they able to effectively relay messages using gestures and their charts?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which emotion words or needs were most challenging for students to mediate? What adjustments (e.g., more visual aids, simpler prompts) are needed for future mediation tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did the "Emotion Faces Chart" project aid in developing mediation skills?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I'm Excited!" theme by consolidating all learned vocabulary and integrating the 21st Century Skills project. The next theme will build on this foundation of expressing and mediating feelings, possibly by mediating more complex social interactions or sharing simple opinions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>